p  against somebody and you can't stand  them and I think it was then exacerbated  by the fact that when when Johnson was  made vice president he had been such a  powerful majority leader and he felt as  vice president that he was left out of  the loop on all sorts of decisions that  that Kennedy was making and he somehow  didn't want to blame Kennedy he liked  Kennedy in fact the two of them Jack  Kennedy and Lyndon Johnson got along  fairly well  Kennedy liked listening to Johnson's  stories he knew what a character in a  rogue Johnson was but then when he  wasn't getting invited to meetings or in  decisions he always blamed Bobby he  thought Bobby was keeping him out of the  meetings and then when Lyndon Johnson  becomes president after John Kennedy has  died I think Bobby felt that somehow  Johnson was upstaging his brother that  he had become such an important  president the civil rights things that  happened under his watch and that  therefore somehow his brothers echo and  history would be lessened and then of  course when Bobby runs for the Senate  and then it's potentially going to run  for the presidency against Lyndon  Johnson it only makes it worse so there  was absolutely no love left between  those two people I don't know you know  it's true my book on the Fitzgerald and  Kennedys ended with President Kennedy  becoming president and it didn't go any  further but they've been so many  wonderful books written about his  presidency that I'd have to be able to  figure out again a different way into it  if I were going to follow up with that  Palm Bay Florida you're next a great  honor miss Goodman I have three very  quick questions up is it can you name a  current senator that resembles a true  Democrat from the sixties and also was a  president Lincoln the is it true that he  was a  the father of the modern day income tax  and it was mostly for defense of our  country not really social social  programs and the last one is um do you  think Lincoln and Mart