he war there was that  natural tendency to surround himself  with the people who agreed with him and  that only means that you fully yourself  even deeper into a whole and I think it  probably has relevance for us today you  know that when things are difficult the  time is then there to reach out to  people who disagree with you who can  debate with you who can argue with you  so that you can see other points of view  and maybe get out of the hole that one  is in what surprised you the most in  putting together a team of rivals well  you know I think the most superficial  thing that surprised me but it probably  isn't superficial is that having seen  the sad eyes that Lincoln had having  known about his melancholy temperament I  had no idea how dynamic a personality he  had I mean I'm sure the Lincoln scholars  who studied them their whole life  understood that but for me he ended up  being much more vital much more alive  I've even teased that he seemed even  sexy in one of the pictures there's this  great picture of him before that  terrible beard that he grew to please  this little girl and and there was a  sense in which he's got a rugged face I  think it's on page 200 he's got a rugged  face there's disheveled hair maybe I  liked his shovelled hair because my  husband's disheveled hair and big hair  like Lincoln's but but you look at that  and that's not the image we have when we  think of him in the marble monument and  then as I read further and I realized  that he was the one suspending  everyone's else's spirits that he could  start a cabinet meeting with a funny  story that he could relax people by  telling a joke and that these jokes were  so funny and amusing and they had  something to do with what was going on  there was a live force in him that I  hadn't suspected I knew I respected him  I knew I admired him but I didn't  realize I feel such affection for him  published by Simon &amp; Schuster Doris  Kearns Goodwin the latest team of rivals  the politi